## 10. CI/CD & Docker (engineering rules)

### Test execution in CI

- All tests run in CI exactly as they run locally — same Maven profiles, same
  Testcontainers images, same JDK version. No CI-only test skips or fakes.
- Integration tests use Testcontainers with real Postgres (matching the production
  version in `docker-compose.yml`). Never H2, never embedded substitutes.
- ArchUnit tests run as part of the standard `mvn test` phase — they are not optional
  and are not gated behind a separate profile.
- PIT mutation testing runs on `domain` with a configured threshold. A drop in mutation
  score fails the build, not just warns.

### Docker image rules

- The Dockerfile lives at the repo root and uses a multi-stage build:
  - Stage 1: Maven + JDK — compile and run `mvn package -DskipTests` (tests already
    passed in CI before the Docker build step)
  - Stage 2: Eclipse Temurin JRE — copy the fat JAR, set up non-root user, expose ports
- The image must start with zero mandatory environment variables (sensible defaults for
  local/dev). Production deployments override via env vars or mounted config.
- Every configurable value (`datasource`, `tenant mode`, `active profiles`, adapter
  toggles) is documented in the Dockerfile and in the repo README.
- No secrets baked into the image — ever. Not even for "local convenience."
- The image is architecture-portable: `linux/amd64` + `linux/arm64` via `docker buildx`.

### Forbidden CI/CD patterns

- Skipping tests in any pipeline stage (`-DskipTests`, `-Dmaven.test.skip`)
- Hard-coding secrets or credentials in workflow YAML
- Publishing a Docker image from a branch other than `main`
- Weakening branch protection or removing required checks without an ADR
- Using `latest` as the only tag — every release image must also carry a semver tag
